  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=6 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=6+3+3+0=12.\n$$",
